## Appendix B — Design Rationale

Why each mechanism exists, stated so a reviewer can challenge it on its merits.

| Mechanism | Rationale |
|---|---|
| MECE, constant altitude, compositional children, noun-phrase naming | Children must be mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive relative to the parent, at consistent granularity, describing *what* rather than *how*, and never a step or variant of the parent. Without these rules, decomposition degenerates into restatement. |
| `subdomain_class` and reuse posture | Satisfiable off the shelf without eroding what makes the solution valuable → generic, so reuse. Simple, mechanical, context-specific logic → supporting, so reuse and adapt. Neither buyable nor simple → core, which is where in-house engineering belongs. |
| Differentiation flag on a core Full Match | If a differentiator is already fully implemented elsewhere, either the classification is wrong, the match is shallow, or a genuinely valuable asset exists. All three are worth surfacing; silent acceptance is not. |
| Four mismatch axes | Reuse fails on implicit and conflicting assumptions about component nature, connector nature, global structure, and construction environment. Functional fit says nothing about whether two parts can share a runtime. |
| Constraints and quality scenarios separated from functional requirements | Constraints are hard filters applied during scoring. Demoting them to after-the-fact risk commentary is how a compliance blocker ends up inside a 90%-confidence proposal. |
| `risks` / `non_risks` / `sensitivity_points` / `tradeoff_points` | A flat risk list loses the two most decision-relevant categories: where one quality depends disproportionately on a single decision, and where improving one quality degrades another. Those are the escalation candidates. |
| Controlled quality vocabulary | A fixed nine-term set makes quality scenarios comparable across proposals and machine-checkable, instead of free text that varies run to run. |
| Catalog-native classification | Kind, type, lifecycle and owner carry real information about reusability. A four-way logical-layer enum applied to whole microservices carries almost none. |
| Multi-query fan-out, fusion, dedup, rerank | A single query phrasing under-recalls a multi-faceted requirement. Fusing per-capability rankings raises recall, deduplication across result sets is mandatory, and reranking the fused shortlist per capability is required rather than optional. |
| Evidence checklist instead of a self-reported score; scoring before prose; off-round ceiling; falsification pass | Self-reported scores skew high, cluster on round numbers, and resist low anchors. Deriving the score from citable evidence before any justification is written removes the discretion that produced the original result, and a deliberate attempt to refute a match is a stronger check than restating why it fits. |
